#:script

###
# Kobo Type Extensions

For this demonstration we will also be using the Lawyer object hierarchy from the 
previous sample. 
###
[public]
delegate:(int x)->void SampleHandler

//declare an interface
interface:PersonInterface
{
    function:string GetJob()
}

[public, abstract]
class:PersonBase is PersonInterface
{
    [protected, internal]
    var:string _job = "No Job"
    
    [public,virtual]
    function:string GetJob()
    {
        return _job
    }
}

[public] 
class:Lawyer is PersonBase
{

    [public]
    event:SampleHandler JobRequested

    [public]
    new(string job)
    {
        parent._job = job
    }

    [public,override] 
    function:string GetJob()
    {
        JobRequested?.Invoke(5)
        return "Law"
    }

    [public] 
    operator:PersonBase +(Lawyer a, PersonBase b)
    {
        return new Lawyer("${a._job} and ${b._job}")
    }

    [public] 
    operator:Lawyer =(string str)
    {
        return new Lawyer(str)
    }

    [public] 
    operator:Lawyer cast(int i)
    {
        return new Lawyer(i.ToString())
    }
}



###
Extensions follow a simple format to define an extension use the 
syntax extension:[type to extend].  In the following sample we are
extending the string type with some more methods, properties, and a
named indexer.
###


extension:string


{

###
This extends a string instance with a property ```MeaninglessLength```.  Access
to the receiver is throught the ```this``` keyword
###
    //extension property
    [public] 
    property:int MeaninglessLength
    {
        get
        {
            return this.Length
        }
    }

###
You can also create named indexers with kobo extensions.  
> Note - with kobo you cannot hve the traditional ```this``` indexers defined
in an extension, only named indexers
###
    //extension indexer (only named are allowed). 
    [public]
    indexer:char Characters[int index]
    {
        get
        {
            return this[index]
        }
    }

###
Finally you can extend a type with additional functions
###
    [public] 
    function:void MakeBold()
    {
        this = "**${this}**"
    }
    

}

###
Extension members are invoked like normal members
###

var name = "Joe Smith"
Print(name.MeaninglessLength) //prints **9**
Print(name.Characters[0]) //prints **J**
name.MakeBold() //does not change the underlying state
Print(name) //prints Joe Smith
###
Kobo also supports updating the underlying value in value types by extending the 
```ref``` of that type.  So extending ```ref int``` will add extensions to int that
can change the receivers underlying value (like ref).  This capability only
works for value types
###

extension:ref int
{
    [public] 
    function:void Square()
    {
        this = this * this
    }
}

###
ref Extension members are invoked like normal members
###

Print("*****")
var x = 5
x.Square()
Print(x)
Print("*****")


